---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Icon from '../components/Icon.astro';
import Portrait from '../components/Portrait.astro';
import ContactStrip from '../components/ContactStrip.astro';
import { identity } from '../data/identity';
---

<BaseLayout
  title={`${identity.name} — Senior Android / AAOS Engineer`}
  description={`${identity.name} is a Senior Android / AAOS Engineer focused on Android Automotive media/HMI, performance, platform behavior and systems engineering.`}
  pageType="ProfilePage"
  mainEntity={new URL('/#person', Astro.site).href}
>
  <section class="page-intro">
    <div class="container about-hero">
      <div>
        <span class="eyebrow">THE ENGINEER BEHIND THE WORK</span><h1>
          Hi, I’m Adrian.<br /><span class="muted">I like to look underneath.</span>
        </h1><p class="intro-description">
          I work on automotive media experiences. The further I go, the more interested I become in
          what makes those experiences work.
        </p><ContactStrip />
      </div><figure class="portrait-frame">
        <Portrait /><figcaption>
          {identity.name}
          <span>Senior Android / AAOS Engineer</span>
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="section container about-layout">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>From Android to automotive</h2><p>
        My path has moved from Android applications toward automotive and AAOS. Working on media in
        a car brings platform behavior into everyday product decisions: how playback responds, how
        focus moves, what remains available while driving and what the system does when conditions
        change.
      </p><p>
        That is the part I keep coming back to. A good screen is the visible result of many
        less-visible engineering decisions.
      </p>
      <h2>What keeps me interested</h2><p>
        Media systems, performance and the boundaries between components. I want to understand who
        owns a piece of state, what crosses a process boundary and which work is actually on the
        critical path.
      </p><p>
        PandaWave is where I explore those questions through Android, Rust and backend design.
        Canopy and canopy-api give the other side of those boundaries their own attention.
      </p>
      <h2>What I’m learning</h2><p>
        I’m moving deeper into AOSP and modern C++. C++ Mastery gives that learning a concrete form
        through lifetime tracing, RAII, generic programming, custom containers, exception-safety
        experiments and sanitizer-backed labs.
      </p><a href="/projects/cpp-mastery/" class="text-link"
        >Explore C++ Mastery <Icon name="right" size={18} /></a
      >
      <h2>How I use AI</h2><p>
        I use Claude and Codex as engineering tools for repository exploration, implementation
        support, debugging and review. The responsibility for understanding the code and verifying
        the result remains mine.
      </p><p>
        I also maintain reusable Claude skills around GitHub and Jira workflows and adapt useful
        workflows across Codex and Claude rather than treating agent output as a black box.
      </p>
    </div><aside class="about-callout">
      <span class="eyebrow">CURRENT FOCUS</span><h3>
        Android at the surface.<br />Systems at the core.
      </h3><p>
        Automotive media is my professional focus. Native boundaries, AOSP and platform engineering
        are where I’m deliberately going next.
      </p><div class="tags">
        <span>AAOS</span><span>Android Media</span><span>Performance</span><span>Rust</span><span
          >AOSP</span
        ><span>C++</span>
      </div><a href="/projects/pandawave/" class="text-link"
        >See that thinking in PandaWave <Icon name="arrow" size={18} /></a
      >
    </aside>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
